WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.800
In 2010, two Harvard psychologists asked a simple question.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.800
They interrupted 2,250 people at random moments of their day.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.800
Nearly half the time, their minds were somewhere else.

4
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.800
In every activity they tracked but one, minds wandered at least 30% of the time.

5
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:33.800
Even happy daydreams didn’t make people happier than being present.

6
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.800
Where their mind was predicted happiness more than twice as well as what they were doing.

7
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.800
And the wandering came first. The unhappiness followed.

8
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.800
“A human mind is a wandering mind,
and a wandering mind is an unhappy mind.”
— Killingsworth & Gilbert, Science, 2010

9
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.600
Meditation doesn’t stop your mind from wandering.

10
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.800
It trains you to notice, and come back.

11
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.800
So, where is your mind right now?
